<Docs
	page="recipes/python"
	title="Python"
	lede="The DB-API drivers, asyncpg, and SQLAlchemy's text() work out of the box; Django's raw() takes a query file."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Triple-quoted strings (<code>"""&hellip;"""</code>) take newlines as
			written, so squill lays the query out one clause per line. A string in
			single quotes, <code>"&hellip;"</code> or <code>'&hellip;'</code>, is
			left exactly as written &mdash; to have squill format a short query,
			triple-quote it. f-strings are never touched: SQL with a{" "}
			<code>&lbrace;hole&rbrace;</code> in it isn&rsquo;t SQL squill can parse.
		</p>
		<p>
			Set <code>pyformat-params = true</code> in the rule when your driver
			uses the DB-API&rsquo;s <code>%s</code> and <code>%(name)s</code>{" "}
			placeholders, as psycopg does, so they read as parameters rather than
			modulo.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>psycopg</h2>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>python</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query finds the
			first string argument of <code>execute</code>,{" "}
			<code>executemany</code>, and <code>executescript</code> on any object,
			which covers psycopg 2 and 3 cursors and connections. No query file
			needed:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="python-psycopg" lang="python" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>sqlite3</h2>
		<p>
			The same methods, with the dialect set to SQLite, which is also what
			makes its <code>?</code> and <code>:name</code> placeholders parse:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="python-sqlite3" lang="python" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>asyncpg</h2>
		<p>
			The default query also knows asyncpg&rsquo;s <code>fetch</code>,{" "}
			<code>fetchrow</code>, and <code>fetchval</code>, and its{" "}
			<code>$1</code> placeholders are Postgres&rsquo;s own:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="python-asyncpg" lang="python" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>SQLAlchemy</h2>
		<p>
			The default query finds a string passed to <code>text()</code>, called
			by that name (not as <code>sqlalchemy.text()</code>).
		</p>
		<Recipe name="python-sqlalchemy" lang="python" />
		<p>
			<code>text()</code> spells every parameter <code>:name</code>, which
			Postgres would read as a stray colon; <code>colon-params</code> makes
			them parameters. SQLite reads <code>:name</code> natively, so with{" "}
			<code>dialect = "sqlite"</code> you don&rsquo;t need it.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Django</h2>
		<p>
			<code>cursor.execute</code> is covered already, but{" "}
			<code>Manager.raw()</code> isn&rsquo;t. A rule&rsquo;s{" "}
			<code>query</code> replaces the default rather than adding to it, so
			this one keeps the <code>execute</code> family alongside{" "}
			<code>raw</code>:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="python-django" lang="python" />
		<p>
			Migrations write SQL through <code>migrations.RunSQL</code>: add{" "}
			<code>"RunSQL"</code> to the method names to format its first argument.
			A keyword argument like <code>reverse_sql=</code> isn&rsquo;t the first
			positional string, so it takes a pattern of its own.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
